];
const intent=`BEGIN;
-- Perform the guarded stock reservation and write order 103
-- and its items through this transaction's owning connection.
INSERT INTO work (order_id, operation_id, payload)
VALUES (103, 'fulfil:103:v1',
  '{"order_id":103,"items":[["mug",1],["tea",2]],
    "delivery":"original"}');
COMMIT;
-- Response: order accepted; fulfilment pending.`;
const claim=`-- Run as one statement: its implicit transaction ends before HTTP.
WITH candidate AS (
  SELECT id FROM work
  WHERE attempt < 3 AND (
    (state = 'pending' AND next_attempt_at <= clock_timestamp()) OR
    (state = 'claimed' AND lease_until <= clock_timestamp()))
  ORDER BY id FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED LIMIT 1
)
UPDATE work w SET state = 'claimed', owner = $1,
  attempt = w.attempt + 1,
  lease_until = clock_timestamp() + interval '30 seconds'
FROM candidate c WHERE w.id = c.id
RETURNING w.*;`;
const complete=`-- After the external call; $4 contains the observed receipt.
UPDATE work SET state = 'completed', outcome = $4::jsonb,
  owner = NULL, lease_until = NULL
WHERE id = $1 AND state = 'claimed' AND owner = $2
  AND attempt = $3 AND lease_until > clock_timestamp()
RETURNING id;
-- Require exactly one returned row. Zero means ownership was lost.`;
---
<p>A transaction can commit an order and still leave its fulfilment undone. The request process might stop after committing but before calling the fulfilment service. A worker might make the call, then stop before saving the answer. Recoverable background work needs a durable record of what remains to do and a way to resolve repeated or uncertain attempts.</p>
<p>Follow Mina’s order 103: a mug and two packets of tea, from <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Building applications around a relational database</a>. The order, items and stock reservation already have their <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">transaction correctness argument</a>. Here the shop must ask an external service to fulfil that committed order. PostgreSQL 18.6 and node-postgres <code>pg</code> 8.23.1 provide the executable examples; the figures are illustrative schedules.</p>
