<h2 id="find-the-gap-between-committing-and-doing-the-work">Find the gap between committing and doing the work</h2>
<p>An ordinary HTTP call to the fulfilment service does not participate in the order’s database transaction. Calling first risks external acceptance followed by database rollback. Committing first risks process death before the call. Starting the call inside the transaction keeps locks and a connection occupied but cannot make rollback recall an accepted request.</p>
<p>Take Figure 1 to its end, then compare the four placements at that same step. Watch the order, the receiver and the evidence separately.</p>
<WorkBoundary scene="gap" number={1} />
<p>A missing reply adds uncertainty to either placement. The receiver may have accepted fulfilment even though the API never saw its response. Likewise, a lost COMMIT reply can leave the API uncertain whether the order exists. <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">Recovering database requests safely</a> owns that second problem: resolve the original order operation rather than placing a new one. Resolving the order does not establish whether the fulfilment service accepted its request.</p>
<p>A direct synchronous call can be sufficient when its failure contract is acceptable: a read-only quotation can fail without leaving required work behind, for example. Required fulfilment is different. The shop promises to account for every accepted order after the caller and request process disappear. That promise needs discoverable unfinished work. Adding an in-memory task, or sending to another queue only after COMMIT, leaves the same interval in which the process can die before remembering the instruction.</p>

<h2 id="commit-the-business-change-and-the-intent-together">Commit the business change and the intent together</h2>
<p>Write a fulfilment instruction to the same database, in the same transaction as the order. Commit keeps both or neither. The worker can find the instruction after the original process stops. The example’s <code>work</code> table is a durable job table: each row names work for a worker to execute.</p>
<Sql code={intent} label="Persist fulfilment intent with the order; stock and item writes are abbreviated" />
<p><code>fulfil:103:v1</code> identifies this one fulfilment operation throughout its lifetime. It is distinct from Mina’s request key for placing the order and from each worker attempt. The fixture uses one tenant and unique operation identifiers; a multi-tenant design must establish the appropriate tenant and operation scope, authorise the job path and keep that scope in receiver keys and lookups.</p>
<p>The payload holds the agreed items and delivery choice. A worker reading only the current order later might see an amended address or cancelled order. Decide deliberately whether the instruction means “fulfil this accepted input” or “recheck current eligibility, then act”. Store immutable input for the first; specify the authoritative read and decision rule for the second. A local recheck still cannot stop an external request already in flight when cancellation commits.</p>
<p>A <strong>transactional outbox</strong> uses the same atomic-intent mechanism to retain records for publication. A job says “fulfil order 103”. An event says “order 103 was accepted”, an agreed business fact that another system may act upon. Choose the meaning before choosing how the row will be delivered. The shared guarantee is narrow and useful: a committed order cannot lose its recorded intent through request-process death. External completion remains outstanding.</p>
<p>Return “order accepted; fulfilment pending”, with an authorised status lookup. A worker can begin immediately after commit, even before the HTTP response reaches Mina. Neither that response nor the existence of the work record proves that fulfilment finished.</p>

<h2 id="claim-work-without-keeping-a-transaction-open-during-the-call">Claim work without keeping a transaction open during the call</h2>
<p>Two workers must not both select an unowned row and assume it is theirs. Use the transaction branch’s <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">locking and conditional-change mechanisms</a> to claim it atomically. A <strong>claim</strong> records an owner and attempt identity. A <strong>lease</strong> gives that ownership an expiry, so abandoned work can become eligible again.</p>
<p>The fixture has five states. <code>pending</code> is eligible when <code>next_attempt_at</code> arrives. <code>claimed</code> carries an owner, attempt number and <code>lease_until</code>. <code>completed</code> records the promised external outcome. <code>failed</code> records a known permanent rejection. <code>unresolved</code> records an outcome that needs investigation or has exhausted automatic attempts. The last two remain discoverable; neither means fulfilment succeeded.</p>
<p>Step Figure 2 through the claim, then compare an ordinary completion, a dead worker and a worker that is merely slow.</p>
<WorkBoundary scene="claim" number={2} />
<Sql code={claim} label="Atomic one-row claim in PostgreSQL; $1 is the bound worker identity" />
<p>The candidate selection locks the row. <code>SKIP LOCKED</code> lets a competing worker skip it rather than wait for that row lock. PostgreSQL documents this as suitable for queue-like work, not as a consistent general-purpose read; table locks can still cause waits. In the native rehearsal, B returned no candidate while A held the claim transaction open. After A committed, B still found no eligible row until its lease expired.</p>
<p>The statement increments <code>attempt</code>, assigns the owner and returns the input. Its implicit transaction must finish successfully before the worker sends HTTP. Return the database connection, make the bounded external call, then borrow a connection for a conditional completion:</p>
<Sql code={complete} label="Fence completion by the claim that actually owns it" />
<p>This design requires the lease still to be valid at completion. An old worker carrying attempt 1 cannot overwrite attempt 2 after takeover; our test observed zero returned rows for that obsolete completion. Zero is an ownership failure to investigate, not permission to issue the external call again under a new identity. If completion itself loses its database reply, read the authoritative work state and reconcile the receipt before changing it again.</p>
<p>A lease is a database permission to change the work record. It cannot kill an old HTTP call. A can still reach the receiver after B has taken over. Use a lease comfortably longer than the intended call allowance, or renew through a conditional update while still the owner, but retain duplicate handling even then. Clock and pause assumptions can fail. The fixture uses the database clock and a 30-second lease as an illustrative policy, not a recommended production value.</p>
<p>Claim only as much work as the worker can start within its lease. Bound call time, concurrency, total attempts and elapsed job age. For a known retryable outcome that is safe to repeat, conditionally return the current claim to pending with a future <code>next_attempt_at</code>, bounded backoff and jitter. Permanent rejection goes to failed. Unknown outcomes without a safe repeat contract go to unresolved. The example permits three claims in total; a separate sweep moves expired third attempts to unresolved, so the cap cannot leave them silently stuck as claimed. Give those records an owner and reconciliation path. <a href="/review/relational-next/connections/">Connection lifetimes</a> owns cancellation and cleanup around these short database phases.</p>

<h2 id="make-repeated-attempts-refer-to-the-same-operation">Make repeated attempts refer to the same operation</h2>
<p>Suppose the receiver accepts fulfilment and returns receipt R1. A dies before recording it. The database still says claimed, exactly as it would if A had died before calling. Lease expiry cannot distinguish those histories. B needs a contract that makes another attempt safe, or a way to recover the authoritative outcome.</p>
<p>Play Figure 3 to A’s exit, then compare the receivers. The work record and stable operation identity are the same.</p>
<WorkBoundary scene="repeat" number={3} />
<p>A receiver with a suitable <strong>idempotency contract</strong> recognises the same operation identity, checks that the meaningful input matches and returns the retained result without applying the effect again. Send <code>fulfil:103:v1</code> for both attempts; attempt 1 and attempt 2 belong in diagnostics. Sending the attempt identity as the receiver’s key would deliberately create two different operations.</p>
<p>Check the actual receiver’s key scope, concurrent-request arbitration, input matching, result meaning and retention interval. “Accepted” may mean queued for processing, while “completed” may mean something stronger. Record the receipt’s meaning, not merely a successful HTTP status. <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/#make-the-operation-identity-part-of-the-data-contract">The request-recovery article</a> constructs an atomic identity, input and retained-result protocol for a relational receiver. A header alone does not implement it.</p>
<p>Our loopback receiver retained matching input and R1 for the test run. A real worker process exited after receiving acceptance but before completing its row. After takeover, B sent the same operation again: two requests, one synthetic acceptance, and the same receipt. A separate route without deduplication accepted twice despite receiving the same identity. These are observed tests of a synthetic contract, not evidence about a fulfilment provider.</p>
<p>Without duplicate suppression, use an authoritative lookup by operation identity if it can settle the request. A missing result is insufficient while the first call may still be running. Otherwise reconcile, explicitly accept repeated effects where the business permits them, or leave the operation unresolved for a person or owned recovery process. Do not blindly retry a second parcel into existence. A completed work record, an unexpired lease or a transport acknowledgement cannot prove that an external effect happened only once.</p>

<h2 id="give-published-records-a-meaning-that-survives-delay">Give published records a meaning that survives delay</h2>
<p>Publishing every row change as an event makes table structure an accidental integration contract. A receiver must then infer whether an insert means accepted, provisional or imported, and whether several changes form one business action. An outbox lets the transaction’s owner state the agreed fact explicitly: event E103, type <code>OrderAccepted</code>, payload version 1, order 103 and its accepted input.</p>
<p>Give each event a stable identity distinct from its delivery attempts. Name the team or component that owns its meaning and compatibility. Preserve the fields and interpretations supported receivers need while old records remain replayable; a payload version is useful only if a reader knows how to interpret it. <a href="/review/relational-next/schema-changes/">Changing a live schema</a> owns rollout procedures.</p>
<p>Figure 4 delays publication until the order’s delivery choice has changed. Compare an immutable fact with an order pointer.</p>
<WorkBoundary scene="meaning" number={4} />
<p>An immutable acceptance event preserves what was agreed. A pointer can intentionally mean “refresh order 103 from its authoritative current state”. That can suit a current-state projection, but it cannot reconstruct the original acceptance after the order changes. In the native fixture, updating the order’s delivery choice left the retained work payload unchanged. Publishing that payload and reading the current row therefore had different meanings.</p>
<p>Introduce an order version when a business rule requires it: for example, apply version 5 only after version 4, or ignore older full-state updates once version 5 is present. Allocate versions under the same concurrency rule as the order change. The claim query’s <code>ORDER BY id</code> does not guarantee publication order: one worker can stall while another publishes a later row, and retries can arrive late. If every intermediate transition matters, enforce per-order sequencing at the publisher and receiver; if only the latest complete state matters, a verified version check may suffice. Versions detect a gap; they do not fill missing facts.</p>
<p>Retain event identities, compatible payloads and receiver duplicate evidence for the promised retry and replay interval. Replaying E103 can repeat the fulfilment effect if the receiver has forgotten E103. Keeping the source row alone does not make replay safe. Detailed retention policy belongs with <a href="/review/relational-next/history/">history and data lifecycles</a>; this boundary supplies the recovery interval that policy must preserve.</p>

<h2 id="compare-polling-with-database-change-capture">Compare polling with database change capture</h2>
<p><strong>Polling</strong> discovers work by querying the committed outbox table. <strong>Change data capture</strong> discovers changes through a database’s change stream; PostgreSQL logical decoding derives it from WAL. Capture can publish the same deliberate outbox events. It can also publish raw order-table changes, but those still need a defined business interpretation and compatibility contract.</p>
<p>Figure 5 follows the same event through a publisher restart. Take either path to the end and compare what its saved progress establishes.</p>
<WorkBoundary scene="capture" number={5} />
<p>A poller’s committed reads see committed rows. Its claim and completion state must retain unfinished publication across restart. A capture path must join its initial snapshot to ongoing changes without a gap. PostgreSQL provides an exported snapshot boundary when a logical slot is created through the replication protocol. Its documentation also warns that a crash can make a slot emit recent changes again. A saved source position therefore needs a duplicate-safe downstream hand-off; it does not establish fulfilment.</p>
<p>Verify the selected capture implementation’s snapshot boundary, transaction commit handling, durable position storage, table selection, schema-change behaviour and required source-log retention. Some decoding paths stream in-progress transaction fragments: the publisher must respect commit or abort before exposing a committed business fact. PostgreSQL’s ordinary committed-transaction callbacks exclude aborted transactions; do not generalise that into “every capture message is already committed”. Built-in logical replication also does not automatically replicate DDL.</p>
<p>A stopped capture reader can retain source resources, while a lost required history can make resumption impossible. Choose this mechanism with its operational owner and test its restart contract. The rehearsal here does not configure logical decoding or run a connector. Capture moves discovery and publication; it still leaves a boundary between external acceptance and durably recording progress.</p>

<h2 id="recover-the-unfinished-order-from-retained-evidence">Recover the unfinished order from retained evidence</h2>
<p>Locate the order, its durable instruction, the latest claim and the receiver’s authoritative evidence together. Work age tells you how long the promise has been outstanding; attempts and next-attempt time distinguish scheduled retry from abandonment. Record phase and receipt without logging private payloads. The <a href="/review/relational-next/monitoring-and-capacity/">monitoring article</a> owns service thresholds and alert operation; these are the facts it needs.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Recovering order 103 from retained evidence"><table><thead><tr><th>Retained evidence</th><th>Next safe action</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Old pending instruction; no claim</td><td>Check worker admission, eligibility and next-attempt time. Claim through the atomic protocol.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Expired claim; attempts remain</td><td>Take over with a new attempt identity. Assume the old worker may still act; preserve the receiver operation key.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Receiver accepted; local completion absent</td><td>Recover the retained receipt, or repeat only under a verified duplicate contract. Otherwise reconcile as unresolved.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Permanent rejection or exhausted attempts</td><td>Keep the reason and operation discoverable; assign correction or reconciliation. A new business operation needs deliberate new intent.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Publisher no longer advances</td><td>Identify its last durable progress boundary and retained rows or source history before deciding where to resume.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>If a downstream projection loses progress, first establish which source records remain and whether replay falls within its duplicate-handling contract. Resume from a known boundary where possible. If the required history has expired, reconcile authoritative order state or rebuild the projection from a consistently bounded snapshot plus subsequent changes. Current rows cannot recreate historical facts that were deleted. A projection rebuild is also different from replaying fulfilment commands: one rebuilds data, the other may send parcels.</p>
<p>Inject failures before commit, after commit, after claim, after external acceptance and before completion. Assert the durable order and instruction, eligible owner, receiver effect count, retained receipt and safe next action separately. For publication, also interrupt the hand-off before saving progress and verify restart under the actual capture or polling implementation.</p>
<p>The <a href={rehearsalUrl} download="background-work.mjs">downloadable rehearsal</a> ran with Node 24.16.0, <code>pg</code> 8.23.1 and PostgreSQL 18.6. Install the pinned driver, set <code>PG_ADMIN_URL</code> to a disposable server where the supplied role may create and drop a database, then run <code>node background-work.mjs</code>. The file documents the optional driver-module override. It creates its own database and loopback-only synthetic receiver, bounds its statements and child processes, then closes sessions and removes the database.</p>
<p>Observed results: rollback kept neither order nor instruction; commit kept both. A process exiting after a bare order commit left one order and no instruction. Acceptance inside a rolled-back transaction left one synthetic external effect and no order. Competing claims skipped locked or unexpired work; takeover incremented the attempt and rejected obsolete completion. Worker exits before sending and after acceptance left recoverable claims. A receiver-side lost reply returned the retained result on a matching repeat and rejected changed input. Without receiver deduplication, two attempts produced two acceptances. The third abandoned claim moved to unresolved rather than admitting a fourth.</p>
<p>The fixture deliberately minimises the order row to isolate these boundaries; it does not re-test stock and item correctness. Lease expiry was advanced explicitly in test rows. The synthetic receiver retains data only for the run, so it proves neither durable provider deduplication nor shipping. No broker, connector, source-log expiry, production cancellation race or projection rebuild was executed. Exercise those contracts in the selected deployment before promising them.</p>

